Let me set your mind at rest.  I am under no such delusion.  I do not
think that the only way to be entertained by a game is to win its
ultimate goal.  Indeed, I wouldn't even say one has to do well at a
game to be entertained.  I, myself, have enjoyed countless games of
soccer despite being a rather poor soccer player.  Further, I wouldn't
even claim you have to play a game in the "right" way to be
entertained.  I've often strapped meta rules onto games to increase my
enjoyment.

I find it odd that you are repeatedly accusing *me* of a myopia.  I am
quite willing to say that a game's object is to entertain.  However,
I'm willing to note that words and phrases in english often have many
meanings.  Saying that the object of soccer is to get the ball into the
opposing net doesn't at all prevent the object of soccer being the
entertainment of the players.

You are trying to build this false dichotomy.  On one hand, the
designer may choose "Entertainment" and on the other "Concrete Game
Rules".

The trouble is, if you want a game, you have to have rules.  This is
especially true with computer games as computers tend to require very
specific concrete rules in order to do anything interesting.  In
soccer, for example, you need to write in code the rules for offside.
It could be that these rules prevent someone from admiring a dandelion
in the opponents net.  This is where Kevin Wayne's wise words come into
effect.

(We should pause here to note that Nethack's source code is provided.
Thus, even if the Cabal of Meanies infiltrates the DevTeam and
institutes the Clock of Death, it would be straightforward for someone
to create a patch to remove the Clock of Death.)

> Solving Socoban

Odd you should mention that.  My objection to Sokoban is the bevy of
special case rules that have been created to enforce playing sokoban
according to the "rules".  I think one should be able to teleport, use
scrolls of earth, and break boulders, all with no luck penalty.  I also
think you should be able to use levitation and flying to avoid the pits
and jumping to avoid fully filling the pits.  If I were writing
Nethack, I would try and avoid such crazy constraints on playstyle.
I'd save my playstyle constraints for a different set of objectives.

